# TEMPLATE ONLY - this file carries no real value and cannot be applied.
#
# Note the extension: `.yaml.example`, not `.example.yaml`. That is deliberate
# and it is a correctness requirement, not a naming preference. Argo CD syncs
# this whole directory and parses every `*.yaml`, `*.yml` and `*.json` file in
# it. As `21-api-secret.example.yaml` this file was a perfectly valid Secret
# manifest named `api-secret` in `cse644-gitops`, so the controller applied it
# and overwrote the real generated Secret with the placeholder string below -
# while this comment claimed the file was never applied. Ending the name in
# `.yaml.example` puts it outside the set of files Argo CD will read, so the
# statement and the behaviour now agree.
#
# The working Secret is created outside Git:
#
#   kubectl -n cse644-gitops create secret generic api-secret \
#       --from-literal=API_KEY="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#
# scripts/04_bootstrap.sh does this before the first sync.
#
# Why it is not in this repository, when everything else deliberately is:
# a Kubernetes Secret is base64-encoded, not encrypted. Committing one to a
# public repository publishes the value. The assignment's security rules
# forbid it, and rightly so.
#
# The consequence is honest rather than hidden: this one object is *not* under
# GitOps control, so Argo CD cannot recreate it and cannot detect drift in it.
# The production answers are Sealed Secrets (an encrypted manifest that is safe
# to commit, decrypted in-cluster by a controller holding the private key) or
# the External Secrets Operator (Git holds a reference; the value stays in a
# secret manager). Both were out of scope for a local cluster; the limitation
# is recorded in the README rather than papered over.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: api-secret
  namespace: cse644-gitops
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: api
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: cse644-assignment-03
type: Opaque
stringData:
  API_KEY: "REPLACE_ME_THIS_IS_NOT_A_REAL_VALUE"
